  "executive_briefing": "In late 2026, the international tax landscape is characterized by the steady execution of the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax alongside major statutory adjustments and progressive administrative digitizations. In the United States, structural tax policy remains stable under newly enacted federal legislation, while regional moves like Portugal's annual rate reductions and Singapore's YA 2026 corporate rebates reshape international tax competition. In digital administration, countries like Singapore are advancing mandatory e-invoicing networks (such as InvoiceNow's new April 2026 phases for GST-registered businesses) to enforce continuous transaction controls. Meanwhile, international trade faces sustained friction from unilateral tariff escalations and intense bilateral trade negotiations.",
